// `Struct.update` — the universal struct patch, whose `Partial<self>` has no
// inhabitants and therefore no representation. See structupdate.go for why the
// lowering is at the CALL SITE and not at the signature.
//
// # Which pairs of independently-derived facts this lets disagree
//
//   - OMITTED FIELDS SURVIVE. `{name: …}` on a two-field struct leaves `age`
//     alone, so an implementation that built a fresh literal from the update
//     alone prints a zero value here.
//   - A NESTED ANONYMOUS literal MERGES and a NESTED NOMINAL one REPLACES.
//     std/structs.nomi states both halves and the two lines below are adjacent
//     on purpose: after lowering, `{city: "NYC"}` and `Address{city: "NYC",
//     zip: "10001"}` have the SAME KIND, so an implementation that recursed on
//     the value's kind rather than on its SYNTAX makes these two rows agree.
//     `zip` is what tells them apart — it survives the merge and is replaced by
//     the nominal form.
//   - THE ORIGINAL IS UNCHANGED. Nomi structs are values; `updated` is a copy.
//     Printed after both updates so an implementation that patched in place
//     through a pointer is visible.
//   - THE ORIGINAL IS EVALUATED ONCE. `bump()` increments a counter and returns
//     a struct, and the counter is printed: building `T{a: v, b: orig.b, …}`
//     instead of a copy-and-assign would evaluate the original once PER
//     SURVIVING FIELD, so this row reads 1 against 3.
//   - AN EMPTY PATCH is the original, not a zero value.
//   - A DEEPER-THAN-ONE nest, so the recursion is exercised past the base case
//     that a single level would also satisfy.
import std/io

struct Address {
    city: String
    zip: String
}

struct Person {
    name: String
    age: Int
    address: Address
}

struct Wrapper {
    label: String
    person: Person
}

/// Prints on every evaluation, which is how the ONCE claim is observed: a
/// merge built as `T{a: v, b: orig.b, …}` would evaluate its original once per
/// SURVIVING field, so this line would appear three times where it appears
/// once. `io.print` rather than a counter because Nomi has no mutable
/// bindings, so the trace IS the count.
fn bump(): Person {
    io.print("  (built)")

    Person{
        name: "Alice",
        age: 30,
        address: Address{city: "Boston", zip: "02101"},
    }
}

fn show(p: Person): String {
    "${p.name}/${p.age}/${p.address.city}/${p.address.zip}"
}

fn main() {
    original = bump()
    io.print("original ${show(original)}")

    // Omitted fields survive.
    renamed = Struct.update(original, {name: "Bob"})
    io.print("renamed ${show(renamed)}")

    // A nested ANONYMOUS literal MERGES: zip survives.
    moved = Struct.update(original, {address: {city: "NYC"}})
    io.print("moved ${show(moved)}")

    // A nested NOMINAL literal REPLACES: zip changes wholesale.
    replaced = Struct.update(original, {address: Address{city: "NYC", zip: "10001"}})
    io.print("replaced ${show(replaced)}")

    // The original is untouched by any of the three.
    io.print("after ${show(original)}")

    // An EMPTY patch has no row here, and the reason is a front-end fact worth
    // recording rather than a gap: `Struct.update(original, {})` does not
    // analyse — "argument 2: expected Partial<Person>, got Unit", because `{}`
    // is the unit value and not an empty record. So structMerge's zero-field
    // arm is unreachable from Nomi source; it is kept because it is the base
    // case of the recursion, not because a program can write it.
    // Two levels of anonymous nesting, and a sibling field patched beside the
    // nested one so the recursion is not the only thing happening.
    w = Wrapper{label: "one", person: original}

    deep = Struct.update(w, {
        label: "two",
        person: {address: {zip: "99999"}},
    })

    io.print("deep ${deep.label}/${show(deep.person)}")

    // Several fields at once, including one that is itself a patch target above.
    both = Struct.update(original, {name: "Cara", age: 41})
    io.print("both ${show(both)}")
}
